{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "82",
    "case_summary": "A 13-year-old girl presented with recurrent fever, increasing fatigue, joint pain, hair loss, and red blistering lesions on her face, neck, and extremities for a duration of 2 months. She experienced one episode of generalized tonic-clonic seizures before admission. Treatment included oral antibiotics and topical hydrocortisone for her skin lesions. Physical examination revealed numerous bullae and vesicles on a background of raised plaques on the back, abdomen, neck, and flexures of the arm and groin regions. Nikolsky's sign was negative. Older lesions showed erosions with crusts, but no scarring was observed at healed sites. There were no mucosal erosions or blisters, no malar rash, and no oral ulcers. Raynaud's phenomenon was absent. Cardiovascular and respiratory system examinations were normal. Abdominal examination did not show hepatosplenomegaly. Fundoscopy did not reveal signs of hypertensive encephalopathy or vasculitis, and the neurological examination was otherwise normal. Laboratory investigations showed hemoglobin at 90 g/L, white blood cell count at 21 × 10^9/L with neutrophils at 2.5 × 10^9/L, lymphocytes at 1 × 10^9/L, and bands at 0.03 × 10^9/L. Platelet count was 20 × 10^9/L. INR and PTT were within normal limits. ESR was 55 mm at 1 hour, and CRP was negative. Serum creatinine was 42 μmol/L. ANA was positive at 1/640 titers with a speckled pattern, and DsDNA was elevated. Complement levels (C3 and C4) were low. Anticardiolipin antibody and lupus anticoagulant were negative. Anti-Ro, anti-La, and anti-Sm antibodies were also negative. Urine microscopy showed microscopic hematuria and proteinuria, with 24-hour urinary protein at 20 mg/m2/hr. CSF analysis, including opening pressure, was within normal limits.\n\nSkin biopsy from these bullous lesions revealed a neutrophil-predominant inflammatory infiltrate in the upper dermis with dermal-epidermal separation. Direct immunofluorescence (DIF) showed prominent fluorescence along the epidermal basement membrane for IgG, IgA, IgM and C3. There was also smudgy fluorescence rimming some of the dermal blood vessels for IgA, IgM & C3.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
